                                                  \begin{document}$${P}_{{adjusted}} < 0.01$$\end{document}Padjusted<0.01 via a one-sided Fisher’s exact test with false discovery rate controlled via the Benjamini–Yekutieli procedure.

Putative mKRAS-VAX-induced mKRAS-reactive clonotypes were defined as newly emergent clonotypes in on-treatment peripheral blood that were preferentially expanded after in vitro expansion with mKRAS peptide compared with irrelevant control peptide. For each clonotype, enrichment was assessed by comparing its read count in the mKRAS peptide expansion library with its read count in the matched control peptide expansion library using a one-sided Fisher’s exact test. False discovery rate was controlled across clonotypes within each patient using the Benjamini–Yekutieli procedure. Clonotypes were annotated as putative mKRAS-reactive clonotypes if they met all of the following criteria: (1) \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
                                                  \usepackage{amsmath}
                                                  \usepackage{wasysym}
                                                  \usepackage{amsfonts}
                                                  \usepackage{amssymb}
                                                  \usepackage{amsbsy}
                                                  \usepackage{mathrsfs}
                                                  \usepackage{upgreek}
                                                  \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
                                                  \begin{document}$${P}_{{adjusted}} < 0.01$$\end{document}Padjusted<0.01 for enrichment in the mKRAS expansion relative to control, (2) at least fivefold enrichment in the mKRAS expansion, (3) frequency ≥ 0.025% in the mKRAS expansion, and (4) absence from baseline peripheral-blood. Clonotypes meeting these criteria for multiple mKRAS peptides were labeled as cross-reactive. mKRAS-reactive clonotypes sharing identical TCRβ sequences with previously published mKRAS-specific TCRs, mKRAS-reactive clonotypes from other patients, or a clonotype found in tumor biopsies from other patients were classified as public.

